- Generic README file covering both VHDL and Verilog release variants.

ARM922T AHB Wrapper
===================

This directory contains the files for the ARM922T AHB wrapper.

The compilation order of the core AHB wrapper files is:

ClockInv.v(hd)        (in global_ADK/verilog or global_ADK/vhdl)
LATR.v(hd)            (in global_ADK/verilog or global_ADK/vhdl)
A920TWrapMaster.v(hd)
A920TWrapSM.v(hd)
A920TWrapSlave.v(hd)
A920TWrap.v(hd)
A922T.v(hd)


Changes in REL01
===============

Enhancement: Improved HBURST signalling
---------------------------------------

When the A922T loses ownership of the AHB or receives a SPLIT or RETRY
response, the affected access is placed into a holding register.
Prior to REL01, when the access could re-commence, HBURST was always
forced to INCR.  REL01 improves this by preserving the information
indicated on HBURST if it is the first access of a burst which is
interrupted.  For example, if the A922T wishes to perform a cache
linefill (INCR8) but is not yet granted the AHB and so cannot complete 
any beats, it will still assert

AHB Protocol: Single cycle loss of grant
----------------------------------------

Prior to REL01, if the A922T lost ownership of the bus for a single
cycle, and in that cycle was attempting to perform a BUSY transfer,
then if the the next access continued the burst it would be signalled
as SEQ (i.e. HTRANS would indicate a sequential trasnfer) rather than
NONSEQ.

Page Table Walks
----------------

The A922T AHB wrapper cannot correctly determine the cacheable status
of page table walks.  Previously they were indicated (via HPROT[3]) as 
non-cacheable, but from REL01 they are indicated as cacheable.  This is 
because page table walk accesses will ignore an ERROR response in the
same way as a cached access, which can ease the design of external
logic which may need to know of this.


Changes in BET05
================

Bug Fix : Lost Data on LDM of 2 Values
--------------------------------------

This affects all versions prior to BET05.  If an LDM (load multiple)
instruction was used to load precisely two registers from an uncached
region of memory, and this load commenced immediately after a buffered
write which was delayed (see below), then the second register would
not be loaded correctly by the LDM.  The "delay" to the buffered write
could be any of:

- wait state (i.e. HREADY low)
- loss of grant on the AHB, or the A922T was not granted prior to
  commencing the write
- a SPLIT, RETRY or ERROR response was issued by the addressed
  peripheral in response to the write

A system would be immune to the bug if either:

- all LDM instructions fetching 2 registers access only cached memory

or:

- the A922T was the only bus master on the AHB during normal operation 
  (i.e. it would never get de-granted), and...
- all memory regions set as bufferable (either NCB, write-through or
  write-back) were zero-wait-state and could not give SPLIT, RETRY or
  ERROR responses to accesses.

It was not sufficient to ensure that an LDM of two registers did not
immediately follow an instruction which would give rise to a buffered
store, as the pipeline delays in the write buffer process would allow
time for other instructions to execute in between the store and the
load without affecting the operations performed on the bus.

The reason for the bug was because the NCMAHB signal from the ARM922T
core does not follow the same timing as the ASB signals, and does not
hold the value for the last bus access when WAITIN is asserted to the
core (instead it advances to the next access).

BET05 fixes this bug by locally extending the NCMAHB signal during
waited accesses.


Changes in BET04
================

Bug Fix: Linefill counter
-------------------------

Legacy code from BET02 (before error support was added) meant that the
burst counter for a cache linefill would be incorrectly pre-loaded if
the linefill followed a waited access.  This would result in 9 reads
being performed on the AHB bus rather than the required 8.  The
ARM922T received the correct data: the only effects of the extra read
would be an extra cycle taken up on the bus, and any side-effects due
to the addressed area being read-sensitive (e.g. a FIFO).

The software workaround was to ensure that only address areas which
were not read-sensitive were cached.  BET04 fixes this bug by
correctly pre-loading the linefill burst counter in all cases.


Bug Fix: Error response
-----------------------

A bug was found with the ERROR response functionality in the BET03
wrapper.  An ERROR response should only be accepted by the ARM922T if
it is in response to a non-cacheable or non-bufferable access.  The
BET03 AHB wrapper therefore only added wait states to non-bufferable
writes when ERROR support was added, since buffered writes did not
need to be held up in order to propagate the response.  However the
ERROR response from the AHB was still propagated to the ASB in all
cases.  Thus a C/B access to the AHB which resulted in an ERROR
response, followed by a NC/NB access, meant that the ERROR response
appeared on the ARM922T ASB interface as the response to the NC/NB
access and so was accepted.

The software workaround was to never access address regions which are
capable of generating error responses with C/B accesses.  BET04 fixes
this behaviour by only propagating ERROR responses through the AHB
wrapper in response to NC/NB accesses.


Enhancement: TIC test performance
---------------------------------

The AHB slave interface of the ARM922T AHB wrapper has been simplified
to remove the extra wait states and decode cycles which were being
added for timing reasons.  This will allow TIC testing of an
AHB-wrapped ARM922T core in a similar number of cycles to an
ASB-native core.  This change necessitated the use of transparent
latches on the slave interface: these are instantiated directly using
the LATR.v(hd) component.

New synthesis scripts are included: please read carefully the README
file located in the synthesis directory as it contains important
information regarding the Synopsys LIB files for the ARM922T hard
macrocell.



Changes in BET03
================

Bug Fix
-------

The bug regarding non-cacheable/non-bufferable writes has been fixed.
Previous revisions of the ARM922T AHB wrapper would accept a write
command from the core, pass it to the AHB, and then release the core
to continue running before the write had actually completed on the
AHB.  This broke the programmers model for the ARM, and could cause
problems, e.g. when trying to clear an external interrupt before
re-enabling interrupts internally.

Error Support
-------------

Fixing the previous bug has also allowed error support to be added.
The AHB-wrapped ARM922T will now accept an error response on HRESP for
non-cacheable/non-bufferable data accesses.  Note that cacheable or
bufferable accesses will ignore an error response - this is a function
of the core itself.

Signal Naming
-------------

Signal naming on the AHB ports has been changed to bring the block
into line with future AHB designs from ARM.

New Port
--------

The HREADYS input port has been added: this is the HREADY input for
the slave test port, and in most designs should be connected to AHB
signal HREADY (the same signal connected to HREADYM).  The HREADYS
port is included as future-proofing for designs which attach the slave
test port to a separate AHB bus.

New Design Partition
--------------------

The ClockInv module has been added: this is a clock gate and is
included to allow clock gating to be predictable post-synthesis.

